  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, it's been a month since the largest oil \nsupply disruption in history and brand crude oil  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7200,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prices are now well over $100 per barrel. \nDriving your car has already become very,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14560,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very expensive indeed. But sadly, I'm here to tell \nyou it's going to get quite a bit worse. You see,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as when after the 2022 Ukraine energy crisis, \nyour food bill at the grocery store skyrocketed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29120,
    "dDurationMs": 9920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this crisis will sadly do the same. Indeed, some \nfertilizer prices are already up by well over 50%.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But because this will only affect harvests in a \ncouple of weeks or months, unlike at the gas pump,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you will only start feeling this crisis in your \nwallet later. So, when can you expect things  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51600,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get more expensive? What types of food are \nmost at risk? And especially, how bad is this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "food crisis going to get? Hey, I'm Yuri. I have a \nPhD in economics. And in today's money and macro  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "briefing, I will answer all of these questions. \nBut before getting into that, we first need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70000,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quickly review why Hormuz is so crucial for global \nfood prices. Because while Ukraine is famously the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78080,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grain basket of Europe, last time I checked, not \nthat many crops were grown in the middle of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86720,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "desert. So then, why is the Strait of Hormuz so \nessential for global food prices? In a nutshell,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94400,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's about four types of ships that are stuck in \nthe Persian Gulf. The first are ships carrying oil  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101920,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and these are going to farms all around the world. \nThe second are ships carrying natural gas mainly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109600,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from Qatar. And then the third are ships carrying \nsulfur to for example fertilizer plants in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117360,
    "dDurationMs": 10400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Morocco. And finally the fourth are those carrying \nabout 1/3 of the global fertilizer supply. The way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these four types of ships will affect your grocery \nprices are as follows. First, fuel costs are one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134080,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the main costs at mechanized farms. And of \ncourse, they're also crucial in transporting your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "food to the supermarket. Second, at a high level, \nthere are essentially three types of fertilizers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150000,
    "dDurationMs": 9840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are nitrogen fertilizers, phosphate, \nand potach. And potach well that is largely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159840,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unaffected. However, nitrogen fertilizers are made \nusing natural gas and nitrogen fertilizer plants  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Europe and India rely heavily on cheap natural \ngas imports from the Gulf for their production. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right now they are facing higher production costs \nwhich will translate of course into higher prices  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for these fertilizers for farmers which will then \nultimately translate into higher food prices.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188080,
    "dDurationMs": 9280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Third, to make all of that even worse, about 44% \nof world sulfur exports pass through the straight  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197360,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Hormuz, which is a major component needed \nto make phosphate fertilizers in places like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204800,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially Morocco. So again they will face higher \ncosts to make fertilizers which will be translated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into higher fertilizer prices for farmers all \nover the world. Finally this region does not only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220640,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "provide inputs for fertilizer factories elsewhere. \nSaudi Arabia, Qatar and Iran itself are also three  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229360,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the top exporters of nitrogen-based fertilizers \nlike urea accounting for around 1/3 of global  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237360,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trade. So the blockade has basically removed \naround 20% of ready-to-use fertilizers from global  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "markets. And then to make matters even worse, \nfarming is an extremely competitive business.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Farms have almost zero profit margins. So \nhigher fertilizer and higher energy prices  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the same time. It immediately makes a lot \nof farms unprofitable, meaning that they will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262960,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have to make difficult decisions. They can buy \nfertilizer at astronomical prices and try to pass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270160,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the increased costs onto the consumer or they can \nplant less and accept a smaller harvest that year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All roads lead to the same outcome, which \nis a higher grocery bill for all of us. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "food price inflation, when can we expect prices \nto start going up? Answering this question is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very tricky indeed. You see, these three types \nof fertilizers are not all affected the same, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are mostly used together at different stages \nof the crop-growing process. And crop growing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306991,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relied on food system experts like Raj Patel \nto give us an idea. And according to him, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312991,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current timing of the closure of the straight of \nHormuz and different planting seasons all across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317711,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world actually creates a predictable timeline \nof food price inflation. If we look at grains and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324911,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bread prices for example, it's a matter of weeks. \nThen if we look at products like egg and dairy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333551,
    "dDurationMs": 13760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's a matter of months. And finally, after about \nfive months, we will start seeing the so-called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347311,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protein spike, which means that pork and broiler \nchickens will become significantly more expensive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353231,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the corn and other feed stock prices will \ngo up and these are used to feed them and so that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360991,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will no longer be affordable. Yes, that sounds \nrather scary. But what does it mean for your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366671,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grocery bill? Because you don't only buy these \nproducts. How much will food prices go up? Again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372991,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because of the complexity of global food supply \nchains, we have relied on expert predictions here,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378031,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which can vary quite a bit and will of course \ndepend heavily on two other things. Number one,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384831,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where you are in the world. Some countries will be \nhit harder than others, and number two, how long  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389311,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the war will last. According to the UK's Institute \nof Grocery Distribution, if the disruption lasts a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396591,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few months, food inflation would increase by \nroughly 1 percentage point due to the rising  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404751,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy costs alone. But if it lasts longer and \nif oil prices go up by 150% and gas by 250,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412271,
    "dDurationMs": 10880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "food inflation purely due to energy could rise by \nabout 2.5 percentage points in the UK. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423151,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is just energy because in the UK most farmers \nhave already locked in fertilizer prices for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430111,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "upcoming year. If you live in a country where \nthey did not do that, then according to Stone X,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436911,
    "dDurationMs": 10240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fertilizer shocks could add up to two to four \npercentage points extra to food inflation. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447151,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "notably, this does not take into account yet \nrising interest rates, which will also greatly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454191,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "affect farmers. That aside, on average, the \ncrisis will likely lead, and this is still very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460351,
    "dDurationMs": 11120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertain, to a grocery bill that is about 3 to 6% \nhigher by the end of the year. So, what this means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471471,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that if your groceries were already expected \nto rise by 3%, because there was already high  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476831,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "food inflation, the war could push that increase \nmuch higher. Your total food bill could now go up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483791,
    "dDurationMs": 9520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by 6 to 9% instead. And such a massive increase in \njust a year could especially happen if countries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493311,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "follow China in banning fertilizer exports \nto protect their own consumers. In that case,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498831,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
